## What to produce

**`<summary>`** — one paragraph: what this subcomponent does when it's
finished, in terms of the responsibility it carries. Not a restatement
of `<techspec>` — the reader wants the one-paragraph orientation before
the detail.

**`<approach>`** — the actual implementation notes: data flow,
algorithms, and the integration points. Be specific about *how* this
subcomponent calls its dependencies — name the functions in
`{{ dependencies }}`'s pubapi you're actually calling, and how you
handle what their `failure_surface` says can go wrong. Be specific
about how siblings reach this subcomponent through `{{ subcomponent }}`'s
own `<privapi>`. Do not invent a capability a dependency's pubapi
doesn't have, and do not describe integration through some other
component's internals — you only get their pubapi and failure_surface,
never their implementation.

**`<tests>`** — this is normative, not documentation (v5 §2.8): named
test descriptions here *are* reconciliation's acceptance criteria.
Reconciliation checks that a test with this exact name exists and
passes; it does not re-read your prose to see if the spirit was
honored. So:

- Every `<test name="...">` names the literal ExUnit test description
  it maps to — write it the way it will actually appear in the test
  file, not a paraphrase.
- `<acceptance>` says what passing this test demonstrates, in the
  reader's terms — what behavior a human should trust exists once this
  is green. "Tests the update function" is not acceptance criteria;
  "an update from a non-owner is rejected with a permission error, and
  the record is unchanged" is.
- Test at the boundary — through this subcomponent's own export module
  (`<privapi>`) or, where the test is exercising the whole component,
  through `{{ component }}`'s `<pubapi>`. Internal-only tests are the
  exception, not the default (v5 §2.8): they churn on every structural
  change even when behavior held, which is exactly the cost AI refactor
  flows can't absorb.
- Cover structurally, not by line count: every export this subcomponent
  or its store touches should have at least one test naming it,
  including the failure paths named in `{{ dependencies }}`'s
  failure_surface.
- No network, no wall clock, no unseeded randomness in a test body —
  injected clock, seeded randomness, fakes for anything external (v5
  §2.8). A test that can't run offline and deterministically isn't a
  test reconciliation can trust on every merge.
- `kind="property"` is required alongside `kind="example"` (default)
  wherever this subcomponent is the event-sourced store family:
  replay-determinism, idempotency/double-delivery, and upcaster
  round-trip are not optional extras there — they're the family's
  actual testing floor (v5 §2.4). Example tests supplement; they never
  substitute for the property.
